## Technical approach and work plan

During onboarding, agree the request, approval and escalation contacts. Establish the current account-access map with TWC and its hosting provider. Record the starting website and publishing configuration, verify the accepted recovery procedure, and agree a representative set of public journeys for checking each release. No hosting migration or redesign is proposed.

For content work, retain TWC's supplied facts and assets with each request. Prepare the requested web, newsletter or social version in the destination's existing format. Keep open questions with the request rather than fill missing facts. Present the proposed changes for approval, carry the approved version into the destination and check the resulting public page or permitted delivery record. A change to a source should flag the affected work while leaving unrelated material alone.

For website changes, use an agreed staging or review path, record the deployment and retain rollback instructions. Check responsive behavior, relevant accessibility requirements and the intended user action. Changes involving external services should include the provider handoff and resulting status. Reports must distinguish successful publication, delivery evidence and any outcome the available measurement cannot establish.

For newsletters and social publishing, maintain the approved source materials, destination, audience direction and scheduled release. Work within TWC's account permissions. Do not change consent, audience or tracking configuration as an incidental implementation step. Escalate inquiries and inappropriate activity to the designated contact through the agreed process.

Monthly reporting should pair completed requests with source and destination references, identify outstanding decisions and summarize the agreed metrics. Quarterly reviews should choose improvements from observed user and operational needs. Avoid speculative conversion claims where the available analytics do not measure the action.

